Treat a vanished UDC as 'not attached' instead of an error.

A dual role controller unregisters its UDC when the Type-C port resolves to
host: dwc3 hands a600000.usb to xhci and /sys/class/udc/a600000.usb goes away
entirely. On the Fairphone 5 this happens whenever the phone is plugged into a
charger rather than a host -- the gadget comes up first, then the role switch
lands and the directory disappears underneath the daemon.

wait_for_state() reads .../<udc>/state with `?`, so the missing directory is a
hard error rather than a state, and every mode switch after that point fails:

  ❌ UDC reconfiguring failed: Path does not exists:
     /sys/class/udc/a600000.usb/state, cannot read.
  ⚠  Failed to set default mode

enable() already tolerates the unplugged case, disable() does not, and it is
disable() that runs first on a mode switch. Nothing can be attached to a UDC
that is not there, so let the missing node satisfy a wait for 'not attached'.
A wait for any other state keeps polling, so a UDC that comes back (cable
plugged into a host) is still picked up within the existing 3s window.

--- a/src/gadget.rs
+++ b/src/gadget.rs
@@ -138,14 +138,29 @@
     }
 
     fn wait_for_state(udc_name: &str, state: &str) -> anyhow::Result<()> {
+        let state_path = PathBuf::from(SYSFS_CLASS_BASE_PATH)
+            .join(UDC_PATH)
+            .join(udc_name)
+            .join(STATE_NODE);
+
         for _n in 1..30 {
             thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(100));
-            let value = sysfs_read(
-                PathBuf::from(SYSFS_CLASS_BASE_PATH)
-                    .join(UDC_PATH)
-                    .join(udc_name)
-                    .join(STATE_NODE),
-            )?;
+
+            /*
+             * A dual role controller unregisters its UDC when the port
+             * switches to host, so the whole /sys/class/udc/<udc> directory
+             * disappears instead of the state node reading 'not attached'.
+             * Nothing can be attached to a UDC that is not there, so take
+             * the missing node as that state rather than an error.
+             */
+            if !state_path.exists() {
+                if state == "not attached" {
+                    return Ok(());
+                }
+                continue;
+            }
+
+            let value = sysfs_read(&state_path)?;
             if value == state {
                 return Ok(());
             }
